  "chapter_note": "This chapter is essentially a narrative-pastoral exchange between the Apostle (Pakhaios/the Illuminator) and a catechumen who complained about quarreling among the Elect. The body consists of (a) a parable of the fragrant tree that releases scent even when burned, (b) hortatory exhortation not to despise the Elect when they quarrel, and (c) a doctrinal allusion to \"the mystery of the two substances\" / Old Man dwelling in the body — but everything is framed pastorally with second-person address. NO cosmological-correspondential substrate teaching. The dualist principle (good/evil dwelling in every man, Old Man in the body, Sin's flesh) appears only as a citation-summary embedded in exhortation, not as systematic mapping. Editorial fatigue confirms: hortatory rises sharply in second half. The whole chapter is a Manichaean ecclesial-hortatory composition about Elect/Catechumen relations.",
